<p class="sst-lead">Make the parametrization <em>intrinsic</em>: treat each airfoil as a centred landmark matrix and
take its polar decomposition &mdash; the very split from the prologue.</p>

<p>\[ X = \widetilde{X}\,P,\qquad [\widetilde{X}]\in\mathrm{Gr}(2,n)\ \text{(undulation)},\quad P\in\mathcal{S}^2_{++}\ \text{(scale)}. \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>To build a 3-D blade from 2-D cross-sections, <strong>interpolate each factor along its own manifold</strong>:
  a <strong>Grassmann geodesic</strong> for the undulation, an <strong>SPD geodesic</strong> for the scale, then
  reconstruct \(X=\widetilde{X}P\) at every span station.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">This is G2Aero. The widget runs the real geodesics (not a sketch): watch the cross-section
  morph above as the blade is laid down span-wise below.</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>The active-subspace idea generalizes too: <strong>active manifold-geodesics</strong> (AMG) replace the average
  gradient outer product with one that respects the geometry &mdash; intrinsic ridges on the shape manifold.</p>
</div>
